# Line endings, settled once.
#
# This repository is worked on from Windows, where `core.autocrlf=true` rewrites
# checked-out text to CRLF. Anything a tool *regenerates* comes back with LF —
# `flutter gen-l10n`, `build_runner`, `dart format` — so after every regeneration
# git reported the l10n and drift output as modified when not one character had
# changed. Phantom diffs on generated files are worse than untidy: they train
# whoever reads `git status` to skip past those paths, which is exactly where a
# real regeneration difference would hide.
#
# `text=auto` normalises to LF in the repository and lets each checkout use
# whatever the platform wants, so the working tree can be CRLF and the stored
# blob stable.
* text=auto

# Dart, and the config that travels with it, are always text.
*.dart   text
*.arb    text eol=lf
*.yaml   text
*.yml    text
*.json   text
*.md     text
*.gradle text
*.kts    text
*.xml    text
*.svg    text

# Shell scripts are LF everywhere, including on Windows checkouts. A `.sh` with
# CRLF is not merely untidy: bash reads the carriage return as part of the
# shebang and of every line, so the script dies with `$'\r': command not found`
# under Git Bash and `bad interpreter` on a Linux runner.
# tool/check_invariants.sh has to run in both.
*.sh     text eol=lf

# Binary, so git never tries to normalise or diff them as lines. The golden
# renders are ignored rather than tracked (see .gitignore), but the branding
# bitmaps and the bundled font are here and must survive byte-for-byte.
*.png    binary
*.ico    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.ttf    binary
*.jks    binary
*.keystore binary
